"Essential code requirements for the reproducible seeds experiment on MovieLens 100K using LensKit ImplicitMF:\n\n1) Data loading and preprocessing\n- Load the file named u.data from the current working directory.\n- Columns: user_id, item_id, rating, timestamp. Normalize ids to zero-based indices if needed.\n- Convert explicit ratings to an implicit-feedback format suitable for LensKit ImplicitMF by computing confidence for observed interactions as: confidence = 1 + alpha * rating. Make alpha a configurable parameter with a default (e.g., 40).\n- Create the user-item confidence matrix (or dataset) needed by LensKit, excluding missing interactions.\n\n2) Train/test split strategy\n- For each run, perform a per-user train/test holdout: randomly select a fraction f of a user\u2019s interactions as the test set (e.g., f = 0.2) and use the remaining interactions as the training set.\n- Ensure users with insufficient interactions are handled (e.g., drop users with fewer than a minimum threshold of interactions in both train and test, or adjust f accordingly).\n- Use the current run\u2019s random seed to drive the split to guarantee reproducibility across runs with the same seed.\n\n3) Repeated experiments with different seeds\n- Run N separate experiments, each with a unique integer seed. Seeds must be fully controllable by the user (e.g., seeds = [0,1,2,...,N-1]).\n- For each seed, rebuild the train/test split from the original data using that seed (no cross-run leakage).\n\n4) Model configuration (ImplicitMF)\n- Use LensKit\u2019s ImplicitMF implementation with a fixed set of hyperparameters across all seeds except for randomness in splits/initialization:\n  - number of latent factors (n_factors) = 20 (configurable).\n  - regularization (lambda) for factors = 0.01 (configurable).\n  - number of training epochs (n_epochs) = 15 (configurable).\n  - learning/solver settings as per LensKit defaults for ImplicitMF.\n- Ensure a deterministic initialization that respects the per-run seed (i.e., set RNG seed before model initialization).\n- Do not leak test data into training (strict train/test separation).\n\n5) Evaluation protocol and metrics\n- For each run, compute recommendations on the test set for all users (excluding those with no test items).\n- Compute NDCG@K and Recall@K for K in {5, 10, 20} for each run.\n- Store per-run metrics alongside seed and K.\n\n6) Result aggregation and analysis\n- After all runs complete, compute per-metric aggregates across seeds: mean and standard deviation for NDCG@K and Recall@K (K=5,10,20).\n- Provide a per-seed comparison (e.g., a table) and a short analysis commenting on the magnitude of variability due to seeds.\n- Optionally quantify seed influence (e.g., standard deviation relative to mean, or a simple correlation between seed index and metric).\n\n7) Outputs and logging\n- Write a CSV file (e.g., results_seeded.csv) with columns: seed, K, metric_name (NDCG or Recall), value.\n- Write a summary file (e.g., summary_seeded.txt or summary_seeded.csv) containing mean and std for each (metric, K).\n- Log the seeds used and runtime for traceability.\n\n8) Reproducibility and determinism constraints\n- Ensure all randomness (data split, any initialization) respects the specified seed.\n- Do not rely on global non-deterministic defaults; set NumPy/Python RNGs explicitly per run.\n\n9) Robustness and edge-case handling\n- If a user has zero interactions in the training portion after split, handle gracefully (e.g., drop that user from evaluation and note in log).\n- Validate that test set is non-empty for the majority of users; if not, adjust by specifying a minimum user interaction count threshold or fallback strategy.\n\n10) Practical constraints and docs\n- The code must load u.data from the CWD and not attempt network I/O.\n- The script should be modular and configurable via a small config section or command-line parameters for: seeds, f, n_factors, lambda, n_epochs, K values, alpha.\n- Provide clear runtime messages and progress updates to aid iterative experimentation.\n- Include a short README-like note within the code or docs explaining the experiment design and seeds rationale."
